---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import CouncilFinder from '../components/CouncilFinder.astro';
import {national} from '../lib/national-data';
import LocalBillTool from '../components/LocalBillTool.astro';
import {localBills} from '../lib/local-bill-data';
import {moneyPence} from '../lib/tax-view';
import BillSplit from '../components/BillSplit.astro';
import {CURRENT_YEAR,yearLabel} from '../lib/council-tax';
import {longDate} from '../lib/sources';
const bill=localBills[0];
const year=yearLabel(CURRENT_YEAR);
const billYear=yearLabel(bill.year);
const retrieved=longDate(bill.source.retrieved);
const parishYears=[...new Set(national.parishes.flatMap(p=>Object.keys(p.years)))].sort();
const parishFirst=yearLabel(parishYears[0]);
const parishLast=yearLabel(parishYears[parishYears.length-1]);
---
<BaseLayout title={`What makes up my council tax bill, England ${year}`} description="Find the council, county, police, fire and parish parts of a published council tax bill, then model how each part could change." path="/precepts/">
<Breadcrumbs items={[{label: 'What makes up my bill'}]} />
<header class="page-head"><p class="eyebrow">Follow every part of the bill</p><h1>What makes up my council tax bill</h1><p class="lede">One bill can fund several public bodies. See who charges what, then model how each part could change.</p></header>
<section class="section"><h2>National Band D precepts</h2><p>All 296 English billing authorities now have a published breakdown naming the county or GLA, police, fire and combined-authority bodies. The parish return adds {national.parishes.length.toLocaleString('en-GB')} local-body records, including four ceased entries, with reported <span data-parish-year>{parishFirst}</span> and <span data-parish-year>{parishLast}</span> figures.</p><p>Search for your council to see its components, individual local precepts and long-term Band D history. National components are area averages; exact property bills still need a verified local schedule.</p><p>Browse <a href="/precepts/parishes/">parish precepts by council</a> for every published local Band D charge and its change on the previous year.</p><CouncilFinder id="precept-council" anchor="precept-breakdown" /><p><a href="/councils/">Browse all English councils</a> · <a href="/data/">Download national precept data</a></p></section>
<h2>Exact local bills and component modelling</h2>
<div class="editorial-callout"><strong>Complete all-band local schedule: {bill.name}, {billYear}</strong><p>All eight parishes and the unparished area, bands A to H. Elsewhere, the postcode tool directs you to available area figures and clearly marks missing local detail. National postcode-level precept coverage is not yet complete.</p></div>
<LocalBillTool />
<section class="section"><h2>{bill.name} local charges at a glance</h2><p>These are published locality totals, not the borough-wide average. Before discounts and premiums.</p><div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label={`${bill.name} locality totals`}><table class="data"><caption>{billYear} published annual charges by locality</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Locality</th>{'ABCDEFGH'.split('').map(b=><th scope="col" class="num">{b}</th>)}</tr></thead><tbody>{bill.localities.map(l=><tr><th scope="row">{l.name}</th>{Object.values(l.totalsPence).map(p=><td class="num">{moneyPence(p)}</td>)}</tr>)}</tbody></table></div><p class="source-line">Source: <a href={bill.source.url}>{bill.source.publisher}, {bill.source.title}</a>{retrieved ? `. Retrieved ${retrieved}` : ''}.</p><p class="caveat">Each locality total includes that parish's precept; the unparished area has none.</p></section>
<section class="section" aria-labelledby="where-it-goes"><h2 id="where-it-goes">Where your council tax goes: {bill.name}, {billYear}</h2><p>For the published {bill.name} schedule, the split of each 100 pounds by charging body and a weekly figure for each band. Both are calculations of the published figures, with the calculation shown. Other councils' bills split differently.</p><BillSplit bill={bill} localityId="unparished" heading="h3" idPrefix="precepts-split" /></section>
<div class="prose"><h2>What counts as a precept?</h2><p>A precept is an amount another body asks the billing council to collect. Depending on the area, a bill can include a county, police, fire, mayoral or combined-authority, and parish or town council element. The site records each published charging body and its area of application.</p><h2>Bundled charges must only count once</h2><p>Some mayoral charges include police or fire services. For example, London's GLA precept supports several services, including policing and fire. Its police element does not apply in the City of London. Our model supports bundled components so the same service cannot be added again as a separate charge. <a href="https://www.london.gov.uk/who-we-are/governance-and-spending/spending-money-wisely/mayors-budget">Source: Greater London Authority budget</a>.</p><h2>Why confirm a parish?</h2><p>A postcode is a group of addresses, not a unique property. <a href="https://www.ons.gov.uk/methodology/geography/geographicalproducts/postcodeproducts">ONS explains that postcodes can straddle boundaries</a>. The lookup provides a suggested locality, and you confirm it against the property before we show a local charge. We do not save the postcode.</p><h2>How local schedules are admitted</h2><p>We need an adopted year-specific charge schedule, published bands for each component, a verified charging area, and a total that reconciles to the council's published bill. A missing component is marked unavailable. We do not manufacture it by subtracting unrelated national averages or infer a parish from a council name.</p></div>
</BaseLayout>
